WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.600
c. 200 BC · Llandough hill, above the River
Ely

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:13.127
Will the weather hold today? Smoke's going
straight up. No wind, no rain.

3
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:15.130
We'll get the corn in.

4
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:22.130
Heads only, mind. Cut them high. Leave the
straw standing for the cattle.

5
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.730
The boy's bringing the cattle up off the
marsh, before the big tides come.

6
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:45.730
Good. Last autumn the water came over the
marsh in the night, and we lost a calf.

7
00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.130
Not this year.

8
00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:53.730
The pig's fat, the store's full, and the
cloth's nearly done for the little one's
cloak.

9
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.672
Listen, little one. Geese, going over. The
cold's coming.

10
00:01:04.712 --> 00:01:07.530
Bring wood in by the door tonight.

11
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:13.219
And before dark, a handful of the new corn
goes down to the spring.

12
00:01:13.259 --> 00:01:15.530
It has always given us water.

13
00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.320
c. AD 200 · The same hill, four hundred years
on

14
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:27.592
See the smoke over the brow? They've the fires
lit up at the stone house already.

15
00:01:27.632 --> 00:01:32.850
Good spelt this year. Dry enough to thresh
before the rain comes up the valley.

16
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:38.185
Then thresh it quick. The quern's hungry, and
so are the children.

17
00:01:38.225 --> 00:01:43.850
Fine red pots from far off, and we still eat
the same porridge out of them.

18
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.451
Call them in, then. There's cloud coming up
off the marsh.

19
00:01:53.491 --> 00:01:55.355
And the geese are fat this year:

20
00:01:55.395 --> 00:02:00.650
one for the turn of the year, and one for your
mother, or we'll never hear the end of it.

21
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.650
Let them run. The hill's big enough for all of
us.

